r"""Source of scripts/content/trc_writing_results_cmelr.json.

TSI course M-412-01 «Trends and Challenges in Smart Systems and Robotics»,
Session 6 «Writing our Results»: how the C·M·E·L·R notes on the 56 papers of
the class corpus become the results section of our scoping review.

Sources (all read before writing, Moodle export of 20.11.2026 and the Session 6
folder on Google Drive):
  * S06 Self-study lecture «Writing our Results: From Reading Notes to Cited
    Paragraphs» (session-06w.html) and its worked solutions (s06w-solutions);
  * S06 Self-study guide «Where to Find C·M·E·L·R in a Paper» (session-06.html)
    and its worked solutions (s06-solutions);
  * S06 Trace table TC-CMELR-TRACE-TABLE.xlsx (406 rows, 56 papers);
  * S00 Citation guide «From reading notes to our review paper»;
  * S00 Formative homework 1 and S06 «Revised theme section» assignments.
Every number in block 5 is printed on the page the sources give; the arithmetic
is re-done in %TEMP%\lt-author-trc_writing_results_cmelr\verify.py.

The course is taught in English, so citation sentences stay in English and the
English term stands next to the Russian one. Decimal point, not comma, as the
journal requires (UK English, «always a decimal point»).

Each entry is (question, correct, [three distractors], explain, usage). The
builder places the correct option at a balanced position and writes the
compact one-line JSON the rest of the tooling expects.

Run:  python scripts/authoring/trc_writing_results_cmelr.py
Then: python scripts/check_quality.py trc_writing_results_cmelr && python scripts/build.py
"""
import json
import os
import random

Q = []


def add(q, correct, wrong, explain, without, with_, benefit, code=False):
    assert len(wrong) == 3, q
    assert len({correct, *wrong}) == 4, q
    Q.append(dict(q=q, correct=correct, wrong=wrong, explain=explain,
                  usage=dict(code=code, without=without, with_=with_, benefit=benefit)))


